(I. LAYING PLANS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Tsao Kung, in defining the meaning of the Chinese for the title of) Tj T*
(this chapter, says it refers to the deliberations in the temple) Tj T*
(selected by the general for his temporary use, or as we should say, in) Tj T*
(his tent. See.  26.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. Sun Tz said: The art of war is of vital importance to the State.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. It is a matter of life and death, a road either to safety or to) Tj T*
(ruin. Hence it is a subject of inquiry which can on no account be) Tj T*
(neglected.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. The art of war, then, is governed by five constant factors, to be) Tj T*
(taken into account in ones deliberations, when seeking to determine) Tj T*
(the conditions obtaining in the field.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. These are: \(1\) The Moral Law; \(2\) Heaven; \(3\) Earth; \(4\) The) Tj T*
(Commander; \(5\) Method and discipline.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([It appears from what follows that Sun Tz means by "Moral Law" a) Tj T*
(principle of harmony, not unlike the Tao of Lao Tz in its moral) Tj T*
(aspect. One might be tempted to render it by "morale," were it not) Tj T*
(considered as an attribute of the _ruler_ in  13.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(5, 6. _The Moral Law_ causes the people to be in complete accord with) Tj T*
(their ruler, so that they will follow him regardless of their lives,) Tj T*
(undismayed by any danger.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Tu Yu quotes Wang Tz as saying: "Without constant practice, the) Tj T*
(officers will be nervous and undecided when mustering for battle;) Tj T*
(without constant practice, the general will be wavering and irresolute) Tj T*
(when the crisis is at hand."]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(7. _Heaven_ signifies night and day, cold and heat, times and seasons.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([The commentators, I think, make an unnecessary mystery of two words) Tj T*
(here. Meng Shih refers to "the hard and the soft, waxing and waning" of) Tj T*
(Heaven. Wang Hsi, however, may be right in saying that what is meant is) Tj T*
("the general economy of Heaven," including the five elements, the four) Tj T*
(seasons, wind and clouds, and other phenomena.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(8. _Earth_ comprises distances, great and small; danger and security;) Tj T*
(open ground and narrow passes; the chances of life and death.) Tj T*
