(9. _The Commander_ stands for the virtues of wisdom, sincerity,) Tj T*
(benevolence, courage and strictness.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([The five cardinal virtues of the Chinese are \(1\) humanity or) Tj T*
(benevolence; \(2\) uprightness of mind; \(3\) self-respect, self-control,) Tj T*
(or "proper feeling;" \(4\) wisdom; \(5\) sincerity or good faith. Here) Tj T*
("wisdom" and "sincerity" are put before "humanity or benevolence," and) Tj T*
(the two military virtues of "courage" and "strictness" substituted for) Tj T*
("uprightness of mind" and "self-respect, self-control, or proper) Tj T*
(feeling."]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(10. By _Method and discipline_ are to be understood the marshalling of) Tj T*
(the army in its proper subdivisions, the gradations of rank among the) Tj T*
(officers, the maintenance of roads by which supplies may reach the) Tj T*
(army, and the control of military expenditure.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(11. These five heads should be familiar to every general: he who knows) Tj T*
(them will be victorious; he who knows them not will fail.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(12. Therefore, in your deliberations, when seeking to determine the) Tj T*
(military conditions, let them be made the basis of a comparison, in) Tj T*
(this wise:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(13. \(1\) Which of the two sovereigns is imbued with the Moral law?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
([I.e., "is in harmony with his subjects." Cf.  5.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(    \(2\) Which of the two generals has most ability?) Tj T*
(    \(3\) With whom lie the advantages derived from Heaven and Earth?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([See  7, 8]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(\(4\) On which side is discipline most rigorously enforced?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Tu Mu alludes to the remarkable story of Tsao Tsao \(A.D. 155-220\),) Tj T*
(who was such a strict disciplinarian that once, in accordance with his) Tj T*
(own severe regulations against injury to standing crops, he condemned) Tj T*
(himself to death for having allowed his horse to shy into a field of) Tj T*
(corn! However, in lieu of losing his head, he was persuaded to satisfy) Tj T*
(his sense of justice by cutting off his hair. Tsao Tsaos own comment) Tj T*
(on the present passage is characteristically curt: "when you lay down a) Tj T*
(law, see that it is not disobeyed; if it is disobeyed the offender must) Tj T*
(be put to death."]) Tj T*
